---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import GuideTable from '../../components/GuideTable.astro';

// Long-term average start/end dates published by the Japan Meteorological Agency (rounded).
const regions: [string, string, string][] = [
  ['Okinawa', 'Around 10 May', 'Around 21 June'],
  ['Southern Kyushu', 'Late May', 'Mid-July'],
  ['Kansai (Kyoto, Osaka)', 'Around 6 June', 'Around 19 July'],
  ['Kanto (Tokyo)', 'Around 7 June', 'Around 19 July'],
  ['Tohoku (north Honshu)', 'Mid-June', 'Late July'],
  ['Hokkaido', 'No official rainy season', '—'],
];
---

<Guide slug="japan-rainy-season" photo="kyoto">
  <p>
    Japan’s rainy season, <strong>tsuyu</strong> (or baiu), is a spell of cloudy, humid and frequently wet weather in early
    summer. It moves north from Okinawa in May to most of Honshu in June and July. It isn’t a monsoon of constant downpours —
    expect many grey, drizzly days with heavier bursts — and it ends with the start of the hot, humid summer.
  </p>

  <h2>When the rainy season starts and ends</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Region</th><th scope="col" class="left">Typical start</th><th scope="col" class="left">Typical end</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{regions.map(([r, s, e]) => <tr><th scope="row">{r}</th><td class="left">{s}</td><td class="left">{e}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">Long-term averages from the Japan Meteorological Agency; each year the season can start or end a couple of weeks early or late.</p>

  <h2>How wet does it get?</h2>
  <GuideTable caption="Monthly rainfall May to August" months={[4, 5, 6, 7]} show="rain" rows={[{ slug: 'okinawa' }, { slug: 'tokyo' }, { slug: 'kyoto' }, { slug: 'osaka' }, { slug: 'hokkaido' }]} />
  <GuideTable caption="Weather rating May to August" months={[4, 5, 6, 7]} rows={[{ slug: 'okinawa' }, { slug: 'tokyo' }, { slug: 'kyoto' }, { slug: 'osaka' }, { slug: 'hokkaido' }]} />

  <h2>Is it worth visiting Japan in the rainy season?</h2>
  <p>
    Yes, if you plan for it. June is one of the least crowded months (no school holidays and no big festivals), hotel prices
    dip, and the rain brings Japan’s gardens to life. Pack a compact umbrella — or buy one at any convenience store — and
    plan museums, food tours and onsen for the wettest days.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Hydrangeas:</b> the season’s flower blooms in June at Kamakura (Meigetsu-in, Hase-dera), Hakone and Kyoto’s Mimuroto-ji.</li>
    <li><b>Fireflies:</b> early summer evenings bring fireflies to rivers around Kyoto and in the countryside.</li>
    <li><b>Escape north:</b> <a href="/destinations/hokkaido/">Hokkaido</a> has no official rainy season and its best weather in June and July.</li>
    <li><b>Okinawa finishes early:</b> after about 21 June, the islands enjoy sunny beach weather (watch for typhoons later in summer).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>See also: <a href="/best-time-to-visit-japan/">best time to visit Japan</a>, <a href="/guides/golden-week-japan/">Golden Week</a> and <a href="/guides/typhoon-season/">typhoon season</a>.</p>
</Guide>

